<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toroodbe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Torodbe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Toroodbe were a class of Muslim clerics and scholars among the Fulbe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, originally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a>. From the late 17th century they led a series of Islamic reform movements, founding states such as Bundu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Futa Toro in 1776 and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, who came from a Toroodbe family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. They defined themselves by learning rather than by descent.</p></div>
